t i know of where patients have  passed away  because the network hasn't quite come  through  the asymmetry i think has been  particularly stark during this crisis  that people who don't have access to the  internet or don't have a smartphone that  they where they can whatsapp somebody to  ask for something  then they've been kind of really left  out of this  whole fight for resources they don't  even get a chance  what we were seeing is people lying in  beds people being  bundled into any possible vehicle that  was available because ambulances were in  short supply  and being taken to hospital for just  that instant hit of oxygen that could  possibly prolong their life  what we saw was heart-rending messages  on whatsapp  where families were telling other  relatives or their friends  my loved one has died which means that  the hospital now has a bed available you  can take it  what we saw was reporters going out on  the field  going out to crematoriums and saying  that the government has told us  40 people died in the city today i am  showing you my camera is showing you  that 500 buyers are burning right now  what we had was crematorium workers  telling us i will go to hell  if i lie to you and tell you that this  is not as bad as it sounds  we went to one of the main crematoriums  on the outskirts of jelly which is on  the banks of the ganges river  there were so many pyres burning the  heat was extraordinary  and of course instead of the normal  morning scenes that you would have  all of the relatives are in full ppe  and no one can hear each other through  the masks and over the sound of the  fires burning  it's traumatic enough to have someone  that you love die but to have to not be  able to save them and to try  so hard to do it is just absolutely  agonizing  so when people started asking for  vaccines urgently  india just did not have the supply to  give them the country of 1.3 billion  people which had so generously donated  vaccines to countries far poorer than  itself earlier  suddenly found that their own population  was effectively naked it it lacked the  protection  that even a single dose of the  coronavirus vaccine could offer  that's a big irony is that you've got  the world's largest vaccine  producing hub but it isn't able to  supply itself let alone the rest of the  world  so many of the poor and middle income  countries that were dependent on  india supplying the kovacs initiative  for instance that's just not going to  happen for a while and they're  really those countries are the ones that  are going to be very hard hit by this  and at the same time you know i think a  lot of western countries have been  criticized for not sharing their  vaccines  particularly of india and and others as  well so you're really seeing this as a  you know kind of part of the picture of  the wider sort of  global inequality that we're seeing  around around vaccines  some of the poorest and the most  vulnerable sections of society  probably lack even the basic health  infrastructure that is a human  requirement it's a basic right but  probably for the first time the  so-called privileged in this country  are facing what the poor indian faces  every day  that not even money can buy you a  hospital bed  and really the second wave of the  coronavirus has exposed  severe cracks in indian society in  indian policy making in india's  healthcare system  that are essential to be filled if we  have to go ahead as a nation  when cases started shooting up and there  were thousands of fatalities  a lot of people on the social on social  media networks  called for prime minister modi's  resignation  and then the next thing you heard was  that the government had asked facebook  and twitter to take down those posts  but there were also instances where  social media networks just  took down these hashtags on their own  and  then came in for huge criticism because  they were acting really out of turn  because  they were actually stifling people's  criticism of the government  and of the prime minister in handling  this massive tragedy  this has been the single biggest failure  of the union government in the last 73  years  and 73 years is since the independence  of india  so we can see the political impact the  political impact of modi's popularity  being eroded  probably for the first time as people  dies people realize that privilege  no longer protects you prime minister  modi was elected on  a massive majority in 2014 and he  repeated that in 2019.  he came in on a very business-friendly  platform and he has made some  considerable  reforms in india like a momentous  decision in 2016 to demonetise part of  the currency and basically  ban 86 of the currency overnight  and it didn't seem to affect his  popularity at all  so he's used to being the leader of the  country that that talks to people about  really big picture issues and and almost  on um on a religious level so i think he  thought he could float above  covid but the fact that no family in  india  has been untouched by this epidemic  everybody knows somebody who's  who's sick or who's died  [Music]  you know there's no way that the  government can hide from the impact of  this virus and as one analyst said to us  you can explain away bad gdp numbers but  you can't explain away  a dead family member you know that's not  something  that the government is going to be able  to cover up  it's unclear yet though whether his  support  has really been dented in the long term  elections are still you know years away  so it's really yet to be seen if this is  a turning point in  modi's support in his political  narrative  whether he can come back from this  [Music]  b point 1.617 the variant of the novel  corona virus  per sequence in india is far spreading  across the world  if indians are not vaccinated quickly  if india doesn't do everything to  contain its spread quickly enough  the risk is that more and more of these  mutations  could threaten not just the health and  livelihoods of the rest of the world  but also immense economic supply chains  india contributes more than eight  percent to global growth  but if you integrate what india does  into the systems of the world  into large banks into large  pharmaceutical companies most of the  seafaring ships which haul  cargo from one country to the other most  of these ships are manned by indians  what will happen to the supply chains  around the world if indians are unable  to get up  get out of their beds go to work be  healthy  think be on their feet enable that  business continues as usual  [Music]  as india's power and stature has grown  over the years  as it has taken on more and more  leadership roles  on the global stage world powers in  particular  the us is very reliant on india as  providing a kind of buffer against the  rise of china  in particular in in the asian region  it's really important that as a  democratic country  on the border with china india maintains  a strong global role and if it's  unable to get this pandemic under  control if it's unable  to boost its health infrastructure  enough to create  a stable environment and therefore allow  its economy to recover  from from the lockdowns then that global  stature that india has  will be under threat it's a lesson not  just for policymakers in india it's a  it's a lesson for leaders everywhere  about the areas of policy making that  need  focus about what could go wrong with  just one single decision  that a tiny virus unseen by the naked  eye  can take down strong men all over the  world if they're not careful  [Music]  [Music]  you

